  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 120 PM CST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nSaturday through Sunday, an amplified upper-level ridge will become \nestablished across the northern United States, with southern-stream \ndisturbances continuing to track across the southern CONUS beneath \nthe ridge. This split-flow pattern will favor generally dry \nconditions across western and north-central Nebraska, as the \nstronger forcing for ascent remains displaced north and south of the \nregion. Increasing subsidence beneath the ridge, along with warmer \ntemperatures within the lower to middle levels, should support a \nwarming trend through the weekend. With 850-mb temperatures \nincreasing into the 10 to 13\u00b0C range, afternoon highs should reach \nthe lower to middle 60s both Saturday and Sunday, which is above \nnormal for early November.\n\nEarly into the middle of next week, upper-level ridging is expected \nto remain persistent across the northern tier of the United States, \nmaintaining generally dry and warmer-than-normal conditions across \nthe region. With anomalously warm temperatures remaining in the \nlower to middle levels, highs should continue to reach the lower to \nmiddle 60s, with upper 60s possible in some locations depending on \ncloud cover and boundary-layer mixing. These temperatures would be \nwell above normal for this time of year.\n\nThe broader upper-level pattern will continue to favor split flow \nacross North America, with disturbances generally tracking north and \nsouth of the ridge. This should limit precipitation chances across \nthe forecast area through much of the period. Additionally, the \npersistent troughing near the Aleutians and Gulf of Alaska may \ncontinue to favor the transport of relatively mild Pacific air into \nportions of the CONUS. While brief periods of cooler temperatures \nremain possible, the current pattern does not favor a significant \nsouthward intrusion of colder air into the central Plains through \nthe middle of next week.",
